@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+add fade-pause, fade-play and fade-seek commands and audio-fade/paused and audio-fade/transport-active properties
diff --git a/DOCS/man/input.rst b/DOCS/man/input.rst
index 7ec9a78..df9eff5 100644
--- a/DOCS/man/input.rst
+++ b/DOCS/man/input.rst
@@ -311,6 +311,36 @@ Playback Control
     Without an audio output, the command stores its target without a ramp.
     Bitstream, DSD and 64-bit integer outputs do not support this command.
 
+``fade-pause <duration>``
+    Fade out and pause when the last ramp sample reaches the audio output.
+    ``duration`` is a full-range ramp time in seconds, from 0 to 60. Zero pauses
+    immediately. Use ``audio-fade/paused`` for the requested transport state;
+    ``pause`` continues to describe the actual pause option during the fade-out.
+
+``fade-play <duration>``
+    Arm a sample-clocked fade-in before resuming playback, or reverse an active
+    fade-out at its current gain. Uses the same duration convention. Any obsolete
+    pending pause is cancelled. A fully faded pause needs no seek; an external
+    pause that retained audible device samples is refreshed while still paused.
+
+``fade-seek <target> <duration> [relative|absolute]``
+    Silence the outgoing position with a de-click ramp of at most 5 milliseconds,
+    wait for those samples to reach the output, seek exactly, and fade in over
+    ``duration`` full-range seconds. Relative mode is the default. Repeated
+    relative requests accumulate against the pending destination, while absolute
+    requests replace it. Requests do not queue additional fade-outs.
+
+    A paused seek stays paused. A play or pause command during the seek updates
+    the desired state without discarding the seek. Fade-in is armed by mpv's
+    playback-restart lifecycle before the new audio output starts; no client
+    timestamp matching or completion polling is required. Failed seeks release
+    the fade hold instead of leaving the output muted.
+
+    These transport commands run on the core thread. An output wakeup signals
+    rendering completion and the core dispatcher schedules audible completion.
+    Ordinary seek/pause commands and file unload cancel transport-owned fades.
+    Outputs without PCM fading support use ordinary transport without fading.
+
 ``seek <target> [<flags>]``
     Change the playback position. By default, seeks by a relative amount of
     seconds.
@@ -2452,6 +2482,12 @@ Property list
     ``supported``
         True when the current audio output supports fading. False for no output,
         bitstream/DSD output and unsupported PCM formats.
+    ``paused``
+        Requested pause state, including a pending faded pause or seek. This
+        allows clients to toggle transport immediately during a fade-out.
+    ``transport-active``
+        True while a native faded pause, seek, or resume refresh is pending.
+        This does not include an ordinary volume ramp or the final fade-in.
 
     A seek discards the queued-completion timestamp but preserves the current
     envelope. Device recreation starts at the last requested target. Property
